abstract: >-
  Code, configurations, archived per-run results and the experimental record for an input-use audit of
  histology-transcriptomics survival models on five TCGA cohorts (equal 20-epoch budget, three seeds,
  five folds): every checkpoint is re-evaluated with one input removed or permuted across patients and
  with a random fraction of patients lacking a modality. The official SurvPath implementation ranks
  patients the same way when its RNA input is permuted (+0.001 C-index) but loses 0.068 when the slide
  is permuted and falls to 0.47-0.52 without it. PathQ-Former, a query-bottleneck fusion model of
  whole-slide-image patch embeddings and RNA-seq pathway tokens trained with learned null codes,
  modality dropout and auxiliary unimodal heads, responds to RNA (-0.023 under permutation), keeps a
  C-index of 0.54-0.62 with either input removed and moves by at most 0.025 when half of the patients
  lack a modality, so one checkpoint serves complete, slide-only and RNA-only patients. With both
  inputs it is not detectably better than an RNA-only MLP or a late-fusion ensemble; its margin over
  the untuned SurvPath is +0.036 (95 % CI [-0.004, +0.071]; paired t p 0.074, Wilcoxon p 0.030 over 25
  seed-averaged folds) with auxiliary heads and +0.037 ([+0.009, +0.065]; p 0.020 / 0.020) without them,
  and no per-cohort difference survives Holm correction.
